      "text": "The rightmost three digits of numbers that are powers of 5 can be determined by finding the last three digits of $5^n$, for $n$ in ascending order: $1$, $5$, $25$, $125$, $625$, $3125$, $15625$, $\\ldots$.\n\nNotice that for counts of $n \\ge 2$, the last three digits will be $125$:\n\n$$\\begin{eqnarray*}\n5^1 & = & 005 \\ \\ \\ \\ \\  \\ (F_{1}) \\\\\n5^2 & = & 025 \\ \\ \\ \\ \\  \\ (F_{3}) \\\\\n5^3 & = & 125 \\ \\ \\ \\ \\  \\ (F_{6}) \\\\\n5^4 & = & 625 \\ \\ \\ \\ \\  \\ (F_{10}) \\\\\n5^5 & = & 3125 \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ (F_{15}) \\\\\n\\end{eqnarray*}$$\n\nThis pattern repeats every 4 exponents. Thus, \n\n$$5^{1992} \\equiv 625 \\equiv 125 \\ (\\text{mod} \\ 1000)$$\n\n$\\rightarrow 5^{1993} \\equiv 625 \\cdot 5 \\ (\\text{mod} \\ 1000)$. \n\nNext calculation: \n\n$$\\begin{eqnarray*}\n5 \\cdot 625 & = & 3125 \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ (F_{15} + 1) \n\\end{eqnarray*}$$\n\nAs $625 \\equiv 125 \\ (\\text{mod} \\ 1000)$ , we have:\n\n$$5^{1993} \\equiv 3125 \\cdot 125 \\ (\\text{mod} \\ 1000)$$\n\nNext factor: \n\n$$\\begin{eqnarray*}\n3125 \\cdot 125 & = & 390625\n\\end{eqnarray*}$$\n\nThe last three digits of $390625$ are $625$, so $5^{1993} \\equiv 625 \\ (\\text{mod} \\ 1000)$.\n\nThus, the rightmost three digits of $5^{1993}$ are $\\boxed{625}$.",
