      "text": "To find the rightmost three digits of \\(5^{1993}\\), we need to determine \\(5^{1993} \\mod 1000\\). The rightmost three digits of a number are the last three digits, so we are looking for \\(5^{1993} \\mod 1000\\).\n\nWe start by using the Chinese Remainder Theorem, which tells us that \\(5^{1993} \\mod 1000\\) can be found by solving it in two parts, modulo 8 and modulo 125, and then combining the results.\n\nFirst, we calculate \\(5^{1993} \\mod 8\\):\nSince \\(5 \\equiv 5 \\mod 8\\), any power of 5 will also be congruent to 5 modulo 8. Therefore,\n\\[5^{1993} \\equiv 5 \\mod 8.\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\(5^{1993} \\mod 125\\):\nSince \\(5^{12} = 244140625\\) (which is divisible by 125), any power of 5 greater than or equal to \\(5^{12}\\) will be divisible by 125. Therefore,\n\\[5^{1993} \\equiv 0 \\mod 125.\\]\n\nNow we need to combine these results using the Chinese Remainder Theorem. We need to find a number \\(x\\) such that:\n\\[x \\equiv 5 \\mod 8,\\]\n\\[x \\equiv 0 \\mod 125.\\]\n\nThis means \\(x = 125k\\) for some integer \\(k\\), and we need \\(125k \\equiv 5 \\mod 8\\). Since \\(125 \\equiv 5 \\mod 8\\), we can simplify this to:\n\\[5k \\equiv 5 \\mod 8.\\]\nWe can divide both sides by 5 (since 5 and 8 are coprime):\n\\[k \\equiv 1 \\mod 8.\\]\nSo, \\(k = 8m + 1\\) for some integer \\(m\\), and substituting back, we get:\n\\[x = 125k = 125(8m + 1) = 1000m + 125.\\]\nThus, \\(x \\equiv 125 \\mod 1000\\).\n\nTherefore, the rightmost three digits of \\(5^{1993}\\) are \\(\\boxed{125}\\).",
