      "text": "To find the rightmost three digits of \\(5^{1993}\\), we need to determine \\(5^{1993} \\mod 1000\\). We can use properties of modular arithmetic and Euler's theorem to simplify this calculation.\n\nEuler's theorem states that if \\(a\\) and \\(n\\) are coprime, then \\(a^{\\phi(n)} \\equiv 1 \\mod n\\), where \\(\\phi\\) is the Euler's totient function. For \\(n = 1000\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\phi(1000) = \\phi(2^3 \\cdot 5^3) = 1000 \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{5}\\right) = 1000 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{4}{5} = 400\n\\]\n\nEuler's theorem tells us that \\(5^{400} \\equiv 1 \\mod 1000\\) since 5 and 1000 are coprime. Therefore, we can reduce the exponent 1993 modulo 400:\n\n\\[\n1993 \\mod 400 = 1993 - 4 \\cdot 400 = 1993 - 1600 = 393\n\\]\n\nThis means that:\n\n\\[\n5^{1993} \\equiv 5^{393} \\mod 1000\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to compute \\(5^{393} \\mod 1000\\). We can use the method of successive squaring for this purpose. First, we compute the powers of 5 modulo 1000:\n\n\\[\n5^2 = 25\n\\]\n\\[\n5^4 = (5^2)^2 = 25^2 = 625\n\\]\n\\[\n5^8 = (5^4)^2 = 625^2 = 390625 \\equiv 625 \\mod 1000\n\\]\n\\[\n5^{16} = (5^8)^2 = 625^2 = 390625 \\equiv 625 \\mod 1000\n\\]\n\\[\n5^{32} = (5^{16})^2 = 625^2 = 390625 \\equiv 625 \\mod 1000\n\\]\n\nNow, we express 393 in terms of 32:\n\n\\[\n393 = 32 \\cdot 12 + 9\n\\]\n\nSo,\n\n\\[\n5^{393} = 5^{32 \\cdot 12 + 9} = (5^{32})^{12} \\cdot 5^9 \\equiv 625^{12} \\cdot 5^9 \\mod 1000\n\\]\n\nWe already know that \\(625 \\equiv 625 \\mod 1000\\), and we need to compute \\(625^{12} \\mod 1000\\). Instead, we can directly compute \\(625 \\cdot 625 \\mod 1000\\):\n\n\\[\n625 \\cdot 625 = 390625 \\equiv 625 \\mod 1000\n\\]\n\nSo,\n\n\\[\n625^{12} \\equiv 625 \\mod 1000\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to compute \\(5^9 \\mod 1000\\):\n\n\\[\n5^9 = 1953125 \\equiv 125 \\mod 1000\n\\]\n\nCombining these results, we get:\n\n\\[\n5^{393} \\equiv 625 \\cdot 125 \\mod 1000\n\\]\n\nCalculating \\(625 \\cdot 125\\):\n\n\\[\n625 \\cdot 125 = 78125 \\equiv 125 \\mod 1000\n\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\n5^{1993} \\equiv 125 \\mod 1000\n\\]\n\nThe rightmost three digits of \\(5^{1993}\\) are \\(\\boxed{125}\\).",
